{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/295732-solstice-holidays-theory-and-seasonal-courts","id":295732,"title":"Solstice Holidays Theory and Seasonal Courts","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Changeling: The Lost"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-10-29T05:34:27-07:00","id":295732,"title":"Solstice Holidays Theory and Seasonal Courts","content":"<br /> <i>Notice:</i> I read whole <b>Changling</b>\u2019s corebook a few years ago, so if I make wrong assumptions, please correct me. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Each Solstice Holiday \u2013 known in modern English as Easter, Midsummer, Halloween and Christmas\u2013 are connecting in reality two of Seasonal Courts, one with it\u2019s primary emotion that its ruling this time of the year. And other is about tradition of the Court, making it very special time. Let\u2019s analyze it closely:<br /> <br /> <b>Halloween (Autumn Solstice)</b> - All Hallows Eve is marking half of Autumn ruling. It\u2019s a holiday of <i>Fear</i>, with all about monsters and frightening \u2013 connecting it\u2019s to Autumn Court. But it\u2019s traditions like taken from Winter Courts \u2013 you are disguising yourself, spending times with as many people as you can to \u201chide in the crowd\u201d. You even repeating ways of the Fae, so they don\u2019t recognize you as enemy \u2013 like with Trick or Treat tradition.<br /> <br /> <b>Christmas (Winter Solstice) -</b> Even if it is middle of the Winter, your all family gathers to share a treats and smaller gifts. You fulfill your<i> Desires </i>by the holiday <i>feasting</i> tradition, maybe not so profound as Spring would really want it, but none the less you are making your something comfortable for it. The problem I have is how this would be connected to emotion of<i> Sorrow </i>it should have. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/confused.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Confused\" smilieid=\"10\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <b>Easter (Spring Solstice) - </b>In the mid of Spring you fulfill your <i>Desires</i> by look for chocolate bunnies and eating as much as you can on holiday table. You also waits for some weird Fae bunny creature to come in your way, <i>hunting</i> in reality \u2013 just like Summer Court does.<br /> <br /> <b>Midsummer (Summer Solstice)</b> \u2013 In this time you are go to the woods, lead by the Wrath's mad thrill seeking. You go in the unknown to search for things Fae and they secrets, confronting with they magic itself \u2013 just like Autumn Court like to do.<br /> <br /> I know it could be a stretch, but maybe those analyze shows some interesting ideas on how Lost will mark they holidays? Or maybe adding some story seeds? What do you think about it?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1437,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","user":"wyrdhamster","date":". 11-04-2014, 02:24 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-03T15:38:14-08:00","id":301545,"title":"","content":"Well I didn't know how sorrow fit in to the winter solstice but you just have to put it in Lost context. Christmas is a time for you to be with your family and friends but changelings don't have that anymore , their Gentry took it away from them. Christmas reminds changelings of exactly of what they Lost, it makes them feel Sorrow. There is also the fact that changelings assume that Santa is probably a True Fae and theirs a better chance of you getting kidnap rather than getting presents.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#b22222\"><b>.</b></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3742-iceblade44","name":"Iceblade44","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"707"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-03T23:22:52-08:00","id":301853,"title":"","content":"<b>Iceblade44</b>, it's exactly what I was thinking about for a few days. I was about to post it earlier, but simply forgotten.<br /> <br /> <b>Feast of the Lost ( Winter Solstice )</b><br /> There is fact that Christmas are the most depressing holidays for people without the families. It'e even truer for the Lost, as not only close relatives, but whole mortal society don't remember them and treat them as total strangers. This generates in them a lot of Sorrow - and because of this, Winter Court invite them on the biggest feast of the year.<br /> <br /> In the local Hallow of Court they meet up. Every changeling from the freehold is invited, even those that normally would be banished for this kind of events. Winter Court makes sure every one will be feed up with dishes of local traditions, clothed, and in the good moods. They even exchange the presents. But after all this cheer, when everyone are feeling they are the new family - Winter's Monarch is standing up and making the speech. He read a long list of those that died or being kidnapped to the Faerie once again in the passing year. Those that become lost even for the Lost ones.<br /> <br /> All of this is to remind changelings the freehold is they new family, no one is truly alone. It's also to make Sorrow from the lost of the normal families bearable, at least once a year. And, finally, to remember all the Lost that even in new lives they lost people, they should grief and memory them. Above all, that Winter's Court mission is important, and the Silent Arrow is not as cruel as many think it is.<br /> <br /> So how do you see this idea?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1437,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","user":"wyrdhamster","date":". 11-04-2014, 11:25 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-04T11:03:08-08:00","id":302454,"title":"","content":"Seeing as how Samhain (Halloween) is neither a Solstice nor Equinox, I think you have some confusion here. Nitpick follows...<br /> <br /> Following is for Northern Hemisphere of course... Flip it for Southern.<br /> <br /> Winter Solstice is December 21st (approximately) - Shortest Day of the Year and official start of Winter.<br /> Autumn Equinox is September 21st (approximately) when the day and night are approximately equal in duration. and Official start of Autumn.<br /> Summer Solstice is June 21st (approximately) - Longest day of the year and the official start of Summer.<br /> Spring Equinox is March 21st (approximately) - Again, equal day/night. and Official start of Spring.<br /> <br /> Samhain is a Gaelic Festival marking the end of the Harvest Season. And it is halfway between Autumn Equinox and Winter Solstice, and occurs on October 31st). By the same token, Easter (holiday/festival celebrating Christ's resurrection) is not on a Solstice/Equinox either, and, unlike the others, is not on a fixed date.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\">The problem with defending the purity of the English language is that English is about as pure as a cribhouse whore. We don't just borrow words; on occasion, English has pursued other languages down alleyways to beat them unconscious and rifle their pockets for new vocabulary. - James. D. Nicoll</span></span></i>","edited":{"userid":540,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/540-tymeaus-jalynsfein","user":"Tymeaus Jalynsfein","date":". 11-04-2014, 02:10 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/540-tymeaus-jalynsfein","name":"Tymeaus Jalynsfein","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"869"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}